+
+Projects can replace these defaults with a `mapstruct.properties` resource at the root of the
+mapping compilation classpath. Each entry uses
+`mapstruct.collectionImplementation.<interface-qualified-name>=<implementation-qualified-name>`.
+For example, the following entry selects `CustomList` whenever MapStruct owns the creation of a
+`List` result:
+
+[source, properties]
+----
+mapstruct.collectionImplementation.java.util.List=com.example.CustomList
+----
+
+The `mapstruct.configurationFile` annotation processor option selects a different relative
+classpath resource instead of `mapstruct.properties`. An absent default resource leaves all
+defaults unchanged, while an explicitly selected resource must exist. Standard Java properties
+syntax is supported. Unknown keys, unsupported interface names, and duplicate entries with
+different values are errors.
+
+An implementation can be declared in the project being compiled or generated by another annotation
+processor. MapStruct waits for generated implementations to become available in a later processing
+round. The implementation must be a public concrete class assignable to the configured interface,
+preserve that interface's unbounded type parameters in the same order, and expose a public
+no-argument constructor that declares no checked exception. If it also exposes a usable public
+`int` constructor, generated mappings can pass the source size as the initial capacity.
+
+Configuration applies to direct and forged collection or map mappings only when MapStruct chooses
+the implementation of a supported interface result. An applicable object factory still takes
+precedence. Concrete result types remain concrete, and update methods continue to use the supplied
+mapping target instance. Interfaces without an entry keep the defaults listed above.
